      "text": "To find the possible values of \\(\\sin x\\) given that \\(\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{4}{3},\\) we can use the trigonometric identities and algebraic manipulation. Here’s the step-by-step process:\n\n1. Recall the trigonometric identities:\n   \\[\n   \\sec x = \\frac{1}{\\cos x} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\n   \\]\n\n2. Substitute \\(\\sec x\\) and \\(\\tan x\\) in the given equation:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{\\cos x} + \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{4}{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. Combine the fractions:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1 + \\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{4}{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. Isolate the secant term:\n   \\[\n   1 + \\sin x = \\frac{4}{3} \\cos x\n   \\]\n\n5. Use the Pythagorean identity \\(\\cos^2 x + \\sin^2 x = 1\\):\n   \\[\n   1 + \\sin^2 x + 2 \\sin x = 4 \\cos^2 x\n   \\]\n\n6. Let \\( \\sin x = y \\) and \\( \\cos x = \\sqrt{1 - y^2} \\) (assuming \\(\\cos x\\) is positive):\n   \\[\n   1 + y^2 + 2y = 4 (1 - y^2)\n   \\]\n\n7. Simplify the equation:\n   \\[\n   1 + y^2 + 2y = 4 - 4y^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   5y^2 + 2y - 3 = 0\n   \\]\n\n8. Solve the quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n   \\]\n   where \\(a = 5\\), \\(b = 2\\), and \\(c = -3\\):\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{2^2 - 4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot (-3)}}{2 \\cdot 5}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{4 + 60}}{10}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{64}}{10}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{-2 \\pm 8}{10}\n   \\]\n\n9. This gives us two solutions:\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{6}{10} = 0.6 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = \\frac{-10}{10} = -1\n   \\]\n\n10. Therefore, the possible values of \\(\\sin x\\) are:\n    \\[\n    \\boxed{0.6 \\text{ and } -1}\n    \\]",
