    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We know that $\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{\\sec x + \\tan x}{\\cos x}$ and we can rewrite the given expression with this factoring identity as\n$\\left(\\frac{1}{\\cos x}\\right)\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)$ and write $\\cos x$ as $\\frac{1}{\\sec x}$\n\\[\\frac{1}{\\sec x}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) = \\frac{1}{\\sec x}\\left(\\int_4^3 {dx}\\right) = \\frac{1}{\\sec x}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right).\\]Then $\\sec x = \\frac{1}{\\frac{2}{5}} = \\frac{5}{2}$. Hence, using Pythagorean Theorem, we find $\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1$ and $\\sin^2 x + \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^2 = 1.$  Finally, $\\sin^2 x = \\boxed{-\\frac{21}{10}}$, which is a negative number.",
