    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "$\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{1}{\\cos x} + \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{\\sin x + \\cos x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{\\sqrt{(\\sin x + \\cos x)^2 - 4 \\sin x \\cos x}}{\\cos x}$\nso $4 = \\frac{(\\sin x + \\cos x)^2 - 4 \\sin x \\cos x}{\\cos^2 x} \\implies (\\sin x + \\cos x)^2 = 4 \\cos^2 x.$\nSo $\\sin x + \\cos x = \\pm 2 \\cos x,$ meaning $\\sin x = \\pm \\cos x,$\nwhich implies $\\frac{\\sin^2 x}{\\cos^2 x} = \\frac{1}{\\cos^2 x}$ and so $\\sin^2 x = \\cos^2 x.$ Since $\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1,$ \n$\\sin x + \\cos x = \\pm \\sqrt{2}.$ When $\\sin x = -\\cos x,$ $\\frac{2 \\sin x}{\\cos x} = 1,$ so $\\sin x = - \\frac{1}{2}.$\nWhen $\\sin x = \\cos x,$ $\\frac{2}{\\cos x} = 1,$ so $\\sin x = \\cos x = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$\n\nSince $\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{4}{3},$ then $\\frac{4}{3} = \\frac{1}{\\cos x} + \\frac{\\sin x} {\\cos x},$\nso $\\frac{9 + 3 \\sin x}{3 \\cos x} = 1 \\Rightarrow (9 + 3 \\sin x) \\cos x = 3 \\cos^2 x \\Rightarrow 9 \\cos x + 3 \\sin x \\cos x = 3(1 + \\sin x) \\cos x \\Rightarrow 9 + 3\\sin x = 3(1 + \\sin x).$\nThen $\\sin x = -\\frac{3}{3} = -1.$\nSo, $\\boxed{-1}$.\n\nTo confirm, we check if these values satisfy $\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{4}{3}.$\nFor $\\sin x = -1,$ $\\sec x = \\frac{1}{\\cos x} = \\frac{1}{\\sin x} = -1$ and $\\tan x = \\sin x \\cos x = -1,$ so $\\sec x + \\tan x = -1 + (-1) = -2.$\nFor $\\sin x = 1,$ $\\sec x = \\frac{1}{\\cos x} = \\frac{1}{\\sin x}$ and $\\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{1}{\\sin x},$ so $\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{1}{1} + \\frac{1}{1} = 2.$\nSince both $\\sin x = 1$ and $\\sin x = -1$ do not satisfy $\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{4}{3},$ we conclude that $\\boxed{-1}$ is the only possible value for $\\sin x.$ A nice problem!\n\nJust to add, this problem is from an American Math Competition, so this seems like a pretty natural thing to ask on there, at least compared to the other silly trig trig questions.",
