      "text": "The given function is\n$$\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{4}{3}.$$\nWe can square both sides to get\n$$\\left(\\sec x + \\tan x\\right)^2 = \\frac{16}{9}.$$\nUsing the identity $\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1,$ we can rewrite this equation as\n$$\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x + 2 \\sin x \\cos x \\sec x + 2 \\sin x \\cos x \\tan x = \\frac{16}{9}.$$\nSimplifying, we get\n$$2 + 2 (\\sin x \\cos x)(1 + \\sin x \\tan x) = \\frac{16}{9}.$$\nWe can solve for $\\sin x \\cos x$ and $\\sin x \\tan x$,\n$$\\sin x \\cos x = -4 \\sin x \\tan x.$$\nIf we multiply both sides by $(1 + \\sin x \\tan x),$ we get\n$$-\\left(\\left(1 + \\sin x \\tan x\\right)^2 \\sin x \\cos x\\right) = -4 \\sin^2 x \\cos^2 x.$$\nPutting things together, we get\n$$2(1 + \\sin x \\tan x)^2 \\sin x \\cos x = \\frac{64}{81}.$$\nDividing both sides by 2, we get\n$$\\left(1 + \\sin x \\tan x\\right)^2 \\sin x \\cos x = \\frac{32}{81}.$$\nCombining terms yields\n$$\\left(\\sin x \\cos x + \\sin^2 x \\tan x\\right)^2 = \\frac{32}{81}.$$\nWe can try complex variables here, but due to the symmetry in the equation, all solutions must be odd. This leads us to $s=\\frac{299-6 \\sqrt{431}}{81}$ and $q=\\frac{299-6 \\sqrt{431}}{81}$.",
